  "packing_impossibility": {
   "claim": "n equal spheres of total volume V cannot fit inside a sphere of volume V, for any n > 1",
   "max_packing_fraction": 0.7404804896930611,
   "source": "pi/(3*sqrt(2)), the face-centred-cubic/hexagonal density; proved optimal by Hales (Kepler conjecture)",
   "envelope_growth_required": 1.1053389142800114,
   "options": [
    "let the cells interpenetrate - what the worked example does",
    "let the envelope grow by at least 1/0.7405^(1/3) ~ 1.105 in radius - honest geometry, wrong biology",
    "stop drawing cells as spheres - correct, and not available to a format that stores a centre and a radius"
   ],
   "what_the_embryo_does": "it flattens the cells against one another. In a mouse embryo the 8-cell stage compacts, which is a change in cell shape and adhesion, not in cell volume. A centre-and-radius representation cannot express it, so the stage with the most interesting mechanics is the stage the depiction cannot draw."
  },
  "provenance": {
   "measured_secondhand": [
    "the stage sequence",
    "the cell count at each stage",
    "the stage times and their unit"
   ],
   "depicted": [
    "every cell centre",
    "every cell radius",
    "the lineage assignment between stages"
   ],
   "derived": [
    "the radius-versus-separation relation, from constant union volume",
    "the furrow depth, as a description of the drawn shape"
   ],
   "the_sentence": "This is a diagram of cleavage, not a measurement of one. The stage sequence and timing come from the source file; every position and radius in it is marked \"depicted\", and nothing computed here can turn a depicted position into a measured one."
  },
  "not_modelled": [
   "cytokinesis. There is no contractile ring, no spindle, and no mechanism: the waist in this drawing is the geometry of two overlapping spheres, which happens to look like a furrow.",
   "the timing WITHIN a division. The separation is driven by an easing function supplied by the caller; nothing here knows how long anaphase takes relative to telophase.",
   "asynchrony. Real cleavage divisions in an embryo are not simultaneous, and blastomeres divide out of step from the 2-cell stage onward. This plan divides a whole stage at once because the source file records stages rather than per-cell division times.",
   "anything about shape other than sphericity. Real blastomeres flatten against one another, and compaction at the 8-cell stage is a change in cell SHAPE and adhesion that a sphere cannot show."
  ]
 },
